'''Grup Mayan, S.A.''' is a [[Balboa]]n specialty chemicals company headquartered in [[Sant Cristòfor]] that processes [[coca]] leaves to produce coca extract for [[HAWT Inc.]]'s [[Coccola]] beverage and pharmaceutical-grade cocaine extract for medical and pharmaceutical use worldwide. The company is the sole licensed processor of coca leaves in the [[Abyala]]n market, sourcing raw leaves from licensed growers in Balboa and [[Tapuya]].

Grup Mayan occupies a unique position in the global Coccola supply chain as HAWT Inc.'s sole global supplier of coca extract. The company's processing facilities in [[Ciutat de l'Estació]] extract the flavour compounds used in Coccola, which are shipped to HAWT Inc.'s concentrate production facilities in [[Okaiken]], while the cocaine alkaloid is separated, purified to pharmaceutical grade, and sold to pharmaceutical manufacturers across Adonia for use as a {{w|local anesthetic}} in medical procedures. The company is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker GMAY.

== History ==

=== Founding (1964) ===

Grup Mayan was established in 1964 by [[Miquel Mayans i Comas]], a Balboan chemist who had trained in pharmaceutical sciences and worked in the agricultural chemicals industry. Mayans i Comas recognised that the coca leaf, traditionally cultivated in the interior highlands of Balboa and the lowland valleys of Tapuya, could be processed industrially to yield two commercially valuable products: a flavour extract used in cola beverages, and pharmaceutical-grade cocaine for medical use.

At the time, [[HAWT Inc.]] was expanding its [[Coccola]] operations globally and required a reliable supplier of coca extract. The existing supply chain, which relied on coca extract from multiple sources, was costly and subject to trade disruptions. Mayans i Comas negotiated an agreement with HAWT Inc. in 1968 to become the company's global supplier of coca extract, establishing Grup Mayan as a critical link in the Coccola production chain worldwide.

=== Growth and expansion (1970s–1990s) ===

The company invested in its first industrial-scale coca leaf processing facility in Ciutat de l'Estació in 1970, with a second facility opened in 1982. The processing facilities were designed to handle both the extraction of flavour compounds for Coccola and the purification of cocaine to pharmaceutical grade, in compliance with international narcotics regulations.

During the 1970s and 1980s, Grup Mayan expanded its coca leaf sourcing network, establishing contracts with licensed coca growers in Balboa's interior highland regions and in Tapuya. The company worked closely with the Balboan and Tapuyan governments to ensure that all coca cultivation was conducted under licence and that the supply chain was fully traceable, as required by international narcotics conventions.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1989, raising capital to fund the expansion of its processing capacity. HAWT Inc. took a 15 per cent equity stake at the time of the listing to secure its supply chain interest.

The [[Camboriú War]] (1976–1977) had a limited direct impact on Grup Mayan's operations, as the company's coca leaf sourcing was concentrated in Balboa and Tapuya rather than in the Potocsí–Balisca conflict zone. However, the broader regional instability prompted the company to diversify its pharmaceutical customer base, expanding sales of cocaine extract beyond its traditional Volisanian and Breislandic pharmaceutical buyers to include manufacturers in [[Burawa]], [[Daras]], and [[Okaiken]].

=== Modern era (2000s–present) ===

In the 2000s, Grup Mayan invested in modernising its extraction facilities, adopting advanced separation technologies that improved the yield and purity of both the coca flavour extract and the pharmaceutical-grade cocaine. The company also expanded its product portfolio to include refined coca leaf alkaloids for use in pharmaceutical research and specialty chemical applications.

The company's relationship with HAWT Inc. was formalised in a long-term supply agreement renewed in 2012, under which Grup Mayan committed to supplying the full global coca extract requirements for HAWT Inc.'s Coccola production. The agreement includes provisions for quality standards, volume commitments, and pricing formulas tied to global commodity indices.

By 2026, Grup Mayan operated two processing facilities in Ciutat de l'Estació, employed approximately 2,200 people, and generated annual revenue of $2.1 billion. The company's {{w|market capitalization}} of $5.5 billion made it one of the larger specialty chemicals companies listed on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor.

== Operations ==

Grup Mayan's operations are structured around two integrated business segments:

* '''Coca leaf processing''': The company operates two processing facilities in Ciutat de l'Estació that receive raw coca leaves from licensed growers in Balboa and Tapuya. The leaves undergo a multi-stage extraction process that separates the flavour compounds from the cocaine alkaloid. The flavour extract is further refined and concentrated into the ingredient used in Coccola, while the cocaine is purified to pharmaceutical grade and packaged for shipment to pharmaceutical manufacturers.
* '''Pharmaceutical cocaine distribution''': The company distributes pharmaceutical-grade cocaine extract to pharmaceutical manufacturers across Adonia. The primary medical use of pharmaceutical cocaine is as a {{w|local anesthetic}}, particularly in {{w|ophthalmology|ophthalmic}} and {{w|otolaryngology|nasal surgery}}. The company's customer base includes pharmaceutical manufacturers in Volisania, Breisland, Burawa, Daras, Okaiken, and other countries.

=== Coca leaf sourcing ===

Grup Mayan sources coca leaves from licensed growers in two countries:

* [[Balboa]]: Coca is cultivated in the interior highland regions, where the climate and altitude are suitable for coca production. The company works with licensed growers who are registered with the Balboan government and subject to regular inspections to ensure compliance with cultivation quotas and narcotics regulations.
* [[Tapuya]]: Coca is cultivated in the lowland valley regions. As in Balboa, cultivation is conducted under government licence and subject to international narcotics conventions.

The company maintains a fully traceable supply chain, with each batch of coca leaves documented from the point of cultivation through processing and distribution. This traceability is required under international narcotics regulations and is audited periodically by the [[Adonian Community]].

== Regulatory framework ==

Grup Mayan operates under a stringent regulatory framework governing the cultivation, processing, and distribution of coca leaves and their derivatives. The company holds licences from the Balboan and Tapuyan governments for the purchase and processing of coca leaves, and is subject to international narcotics conventions administered by the [[Adonian Community]].

The company's pharmaceutical-grade cocaine production is monitored by the Adonian Community's narcotics control body, which tracks the global production and distribution of controlled substances. All shipments of pharmaceutical cocaine are accompanied by export licences and end-user certificates verifying that the product is destined for legitimate pharmaceutical manufacturing.

== Ownership ==

Grup Mayan's ownership structure reflects its position as a critical supplier in the Coccola supply chain and its status as a publicly traded specialty chemicals company:

* [[HAWT Inc.]]: 15 per cent — a strategic stake held by the brand owner to secure its coca extract supply chain
* Public float: 85 per cent — traded on the Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor

The company is professionally managed, with the chairman and CEO appointed from the pharmaceutical and chemicals industry. The Mayans family, descendants of the founder, retains a small shareholding but has no management role.

